WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:08.330
If you've been watching this show for a while,
you know some of my favorite guests include

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:14.190
people who are firmly on the political left
like Sam Harris, Bret Weinstein and Eric Weinstein,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.570
some people on the political right such as
Ben Shapiro, Dennis Prager and Larry Elder,

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.060
as well as some people who are hard to place
on the conventional spectrum, such as Jordan

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.770
Peterson, Phil DeFranco and Brendan O'Neill.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.770
Of course if you've been watching for a while
you also know I think the Left Right paradigm

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:35.180
of politics is becoming increasingly meaningless
and the new, more relevant spectrum is one

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.030
of personal freedom v. state power.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:42.390
You are either for a society based on laws
which treat individual people equally with

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:48.500
minimal involvement in their day to day lives,
or you prefer a society which treats certain

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.680
groups differently based on immutable characteristics
and thus the government plays a major role

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.910
in whether they succeed or fail.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:03.079
The simplest way to say this is you're either
for your own determination, or you want to

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:04.730
hand it off to someone else.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.890
If I've changed on anything in the past few
years, its that I consistently find myself

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:14.870
agreeing with the people who fight for individual
choice over governmental authority.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:19.750
If you watch cable news or really listen to
any of the mainstream media, this conversation

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:25.060
about your ability to live your life as you
see fit, which I believe to be the most important

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.909
conversation happening right now, is barely
happening at all.

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:33.710
Actually, I believe almost nothing of real
value is happening anywhere in mainstream

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:38.530
media . Cable news is force feeding you an
endless back and forth by pundits who consistently

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.740
get everything wrong...rememeber Hillary's
99% chance of winning in 2016....while at

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.189
the same time they mask their opinions as
facts and argue about the endless minutia

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:55.090
of a broken Washington DC instead of arming
you with real knowledge and new ideas.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.621
I don't know that there's anyone specifically
to blame for this other than mainstream media

00:01:59.621 --> 00:02:05.560
is based on an old dying model that is being
replaced by new media and new technology so

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.690
quickly that its fualts are becoming glaringly
obvious.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.980
Fortunately, thanks to YouTube, podcasting
and however else you get shows like this one,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:20.030
the mainstream media's stranglehold on information,
which is really a stranglehold on your ability

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:25.530
to think clearly about the issues of the day,
is crumbling at an incredible rate.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.400
Now the question is who and what will rise
to replace it.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.220
A few months ago, one of my favorite people
to sit across this table from, Eric Weinstein,

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:37.470
came up with the phrase 'Intellectual Dark
Web' to describe this eclectic mix of people

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:44.810
from Harris to Shapiro to Weinstein to Petersen,
who are figuring out ways to have the important

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.680
and often dangerous conversations that are
completely ignored by the mainstream.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.360
It's why I would argue this collection of
people are actually more influential at this

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.170
point than whatever collection of cable news
pundits we could be compared to.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:01.500
And it's not just those names I mentioned
above, but people like Joe Rogan, who is absolutely

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.950
killing it on his podcast every week.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.340
It's people like Christina Hoff Sommers who
is fighting the intersectionality of third

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.810
wave feminism while still holding hope for
feminism's original ambitions of equality

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:14.920
of opportunity.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.610
It's people like James Damore and Lindsay
Shepherd who are fighting for the true diversity

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.050
of ideas at Google and at Universities.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.989
This wide tent of people we're talking about
have tremendous political differences when

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:32.580
it comes to taxes, abortion, foreign policy
and more, but we've all committed ourselves

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.420
to fighting for our ideas honestly and passionately,
but more importantly we've committed to the

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:44.000
open exchange of ideas and not silencing our
opponents, no matter how many times they refuse

00:03:44.000 --> 00:05:38.770
to extend that same courtesy 

00:05:38.770 --> 00:06:11.990
to us.

